Given n non-negative integers representing an elevation map where the width of each bar is 1, 
compute how much water it can trap after raining.
https://leetcode.com/problems/trapping-rain-water/description/
Example 1:
Input: height = [0,1,0,2,1,0,1,3,2,1,2,1]
Output: 6
Explanation: The above elevation map (black section) is represented by array [0,1,0,2,1,0,1,3,2,1,2,1].
In this case, 6 units of rain water (blue section) are being trapped.
Example 2:
Input: height = [4,2,0,3,2,5]
Output: 9
Constraints:
n == height.length
1 <= n <= 2 * 104
0 <= height[i] <= 105
===========================================
Think of water as filling a valley
Index: 0 1 2
Height: 2 0 3
At index 1, height = 0
To the left = 2
To the right = 3
Water can only rise until it touches a wall.
Left wall = 2
Right wall = 3
Water can’t be taller than the shorter wall, otherwise it would spill over.
So the water level = min(2, 3) = 2
But the floor is height 0
So water trapped = water level - floor = 2 - 0 = 2
Another example:
Index: 0 1 2 3
Height: 1 3 2 4
Check index 2, height = 2
Left max = 3
Right max = 4
Water level = min(3, 4) = 3
Water trapped = 3 - 2 = 1
The formula in words
At any block:
Look at the tallest block to the left → this sets a “left wall”
Look at the tallest block to the right → this sets a “right wall”
Water can rise only to the shorter wall → min(leftMax, rightMax)
Remove the block’s own height → water above it = min(leftMax, rightMax) - height[i]

Key takeaway
min(leftMax, rightMax) = highest water level at that index
Subtract height[i] = actual water above the block
If height[i] is already taller than water level → no water → negative → take 0

What decides water at an index?
Water trapped at an index depends on two things:
Tallest block to the left (leftMax)
Tallest block to the right (rightMax)
Water level at this block = min(leftMax, rightMax)
Why min? Because water can’t rise above the shorter wall, otherwise it would spill.
Then actual water trapped = water level - height at that index
If the block is already as tall as the water level → trapped water = 0

Why don’t we need both sides every time in two-pointer approach?
In brute force, yes, you check leftMax and rightMax for every block.
In two-pointer optimized method, we already keep track of leftMax and rightMax while moving pointers:
Left pointer starts at 0 → moves right
Right pointer starts at n-1 → moves left
At each step, we move the smaller height pointer (more on that next)
This works because the pointer with smaller height limits the water at that side.
So we don’t need to scan the entire left and right for each index — we carry the max as we go.


Why do we move the smaller side?
Correct idea, let me clarify:
Water trapped depends on the shorter wall.
If height[left] < height[right]:
Left is the limiting wall
Moving left might find a taller wall → water trapped could increase
Moving right won’t help, because left wall is still too small → water level still limited by left
Similarly, if height[right] < height[left] → move right
Intuition: You always try to increase the limiting wall to trap more water while decreasing width.
Visual analogy
Heights = [1, 0, 2]
L       R
Left wall = 1, Right wall = 2
Water trapped at index 1 = min(1,2) - 0 = 1
Since left < right, move left pointer → try to find taller wall on left
If you moved right instead, it wouldn’t help, because water level still limited by left wall = 1

Water at an index: min(leftMax, rightMax) - height[i]
Two-pointer trick: we keep track of leftMax and rightMax without scanning every time
Move smaller side: because it limits water, moving it gives a chance to trap more water
